cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  Decomposing U.S. Crude Death Rates, 2010-2024: Population Aging
  Dominates, and the Denominator Is Not Stable
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Finberg
    given-names: Terry
    # Replace with your ORCID iD once registered, in the form
    # https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000 -- then delete this comment.
    # An unfilled placeholder is better left absent than left wrong, so the
    # field is commented out rather than carrying a fake identifier.
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/terryfinberg/mortality-analysis"
abstract: >-
  A reproducible analysis of United States mortality, 2010-2024, with two
  contributions. It decomposes the change in the crude death rate into an
  age-specific mortality component and a population age-structure component
  using the Kitagawa method, finding that the age-structure component exceeds
  the entire observed rise while age-specific mortality improved; it also
  estimates pandemic-era excess mortality against a baseline projected from the
  pre-pandemic trend in the age-adjusted rate, and characterises the age
  distribution of COVID-19 deaths. Separately it measures the denominator those
  rates are divided by: four vintage boundaries within a single fifteen-year
  series are quantified rather than assumed, one of them -- the bridged-race to
  single-race seam -- at exactly zero, while the Vintage 2024 restatement of
  2023 accounts for roughly a quarter of the published 2023-to-2024 crude-rate
  decline, most of it booking as a spurious mortality improvement.
  Inputs are four committed CDC WONDER exports and three Census population
  vintages, each carrying its query footer or
  provenance record and a SHA-256 verified by the test suite. Every value in the
  manuscript is generated by the code and substituted into the template, and
  every data row carries a human attestation that no automated path in the
  repository is able to write.
keywords:
  - mortality
  - crude death rate
  - Kitagawa decomposition
  - age standardization
  - excess mortality
  - population aging
  - demography
  - reproducible research
  - CDC WONDER
  - United States
license: BSD-3-Clause
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-31"
